# XSH readdir() and readdir_r()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XSH.readdir.001
anchor     functions/readdir.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       For a directory holding files named "plain", "with space",
           "tab\tname", "x.y.z" and a name of bytes 0xc3 0xa9, readdir()
           returns each name once, byte for byte, and no other entry but
           dot and dot-dot (at most once each).
test       t/001-names.c

assertion  XSH.readdir.002
anchor     functions/readdir.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       For a directory holding a file f, a directory s and a symbolic link
           sl to f, each entry's d_ino equals st_ino from lstat() of that
           name.
test       t/002-d-ino.c

assertion  XSH.readdir.003
anchor     functions/readdir.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       After the last entry, readdir() returns a null pointer and leaves
           errno as it was (set to EDOM before the call), and does so again
           on a further call.
test       t/003-end-errno.c

assertion  XSH.readdir.004
anchor     functions/readdir.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With two streams of different directories, the entry readdir()
           returned for the first still holds the same name after readdir()
           on the second.
test       t/004-streams-independent.c

assertion  XSH.readdir.005
anchor     functions/readdir.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       With the directory's st_atim set to 2001-09-09 (older than its
           st_mtim), reading it to the end with readdir() and closing the
           stream leaves st_atim later than that.
nomutant   An interposed function cannot keep the kernel from marking
           the access; no planted fault applies.
test       t/005-atime.c

assertion  XSH.readdir.006
anchor     functions/readdir.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       readdir_r(d, &ent, &res) returns 0 with res == &ent for each of a, b
           and c (and dot and dot-dot), then returns 0 with res a null
           pointer.
note       readdir_r() is obsolescent (OB) but still required.
test       t/006-readdir-r.c
